*.min.js

*.min.css

*.map

Some projects intentionally track built assets and source maps. Verify their

role before excluding them.

----------------------------------------------------

27. MEDORBITS PRESERVATION GUIDANCE

----------------------------------------------------

Git tracks files by default unless a matching ignore rule excludes them.

Do not add broad exclusions for these maintained project resources:

- HTML, CSS, JavaScript, TypeScript and Python source

- /pages/, /assets/, /public/assets/ and public website files

- Official public documents, brochures and approved media

- JSON data, schemas and sanitized fixtures

- Tests, test configuration and test helpers

- README, LICENSE, SECURITY and contribution documentation

- package.json and package-lock.json

- Approved dependency lockfiles

- CI workflows under .github/workflows/

- .husky hook scripts and documentation

- Reviewed deployment manifests and Dockerfiles

- Database migrations, schemas and development fixtures

- Required runtime configuration such as .nvmrc

This section intentionally does not use negation patterns to force-tr

track files. Negation rules cannot restore files when a parent directory

remains excluded, and ignore rules never automatically add files to Git.

----------------------------------------------------

28. MAINTENANCE NOTES

----------------------------------------------------

Inspect why a file is ignored:

git check-ignore -v path/to/file

Inspect tracked files:

git ls-files

Inspect working-tree changes:

git status –short

Inspect ignored, untracked files:

git status –short –ignored

Remember:

- .gitignore does not untrack files already committed.

- Removing tracked files from Git’s index is a separate, deliberate action.

- Never delete project files solely because they match an ignore pattern.

- Rotate exposed secrets and assess Git history after a credential leak.

==============================================================================

END OF MEDORBITS ROOT GIT IGNORE POLICY

